// Reference query. Not deployed in any employer environment.
// Title: Recovery inhibition on Windows workstations and servers (shadow copy deletion, backup catalog deletion, boot recovery or Windows Recovery Environment disabled)
// Status: untested template
// ATT&CK: T1490 (Inhibit System Recovery); ATT&CK v19.2
// Data sources: Microsoft Defender XDR advanced hunting, DeviceProcessEvents (Microsoft Defender for Endpoint)
// Licensing assumptions: Defender for Endpoint on Windows workstations (scenario A-05, Microsoft 365 E5). Windows servers need separate server licensing (Microsoft lists Defender for Servers Plan 1 or 2, or Defender for Endpoint Server); this pack assumes Contoso holds it for the servers in PEP-HOST's zones (Z-CLIN-APP, Z-CORP-APP, Z-MGMT). Z-T0 servers (the domain controllers, IDS-SYNC, and the PIP-PKI issuing CAs) wait on the open design question in threat-model.md TM-A4 and section 9 item 8.
// Rule ID: DX-01 | Platform: Defender XDR advanced hunting, custom detection candidate | Suggested severity: High
// Tactic: Impact (TA0040)
// Answers: detection-priorities.md item 1 (Priority 1); attack-paths.md AP-4 step 10 and the on-premises part of AP-5 step 9
// Architecture context: PEP-HOST, PIP-EDR, SVC-BACKUP (requirements set in ADR-009, implementation not designed, assumption A-19), residual risk RR-11; risk register R-03 and R-05
// Fictional scenario: Contoso Regional Health (fictional). Reference portfolio content.
//
// Purpose
//   Find built-in Windows utilities used to delete volume shadow copies, delete the Windows Backup
//   catalog, turn off automatic boot recovery, or disable the Windows Recovery Environment (WinRE).
//   Sector reporting shows adversaries destroy recovery capability before or during encryption, and
//   Contoso's recovery capability is unverified (RR-11), so this is the last high-confidence signal
//   before impact on clinical systems.
//
// Logic
//   1. Process creations for vssadmin, wmic, wbadmin, bcdedit, reagentc, and PowerShell (Windows
//      PowerShell and PowerShell 7).
//   2. Command line classified into one behavior per row (shadow copy deletion, shadow storage resize,
//      backup catalog deletion, boot recovery disabled, WinRE disabled, PowerShell WMI or CIM shadow
//      copy deletion). The image is taken from the original file name when Defender records one, so a
//      renamed utility is still classified. PowerShell issue #14614 reports that value as pwsh.dll for
//      pwsh.exe, so the PowerShell branch accepts pwsh.exe and pwsh.dll. The reagentc.exe original file
//      name follows SigmaHQ rule "Windows Recovery Environment Disabled Via Reagentc"
//      (id db1c21e4-cd66-4b4e-85ca-590f0780529c) by Daniel Koifman (KoifSec), Michael Vilshin, licensed
//      under the Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 (https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License);
//      link in References.
//   3. Rows rolled up per device. Two or more distinct behaviors on one device in the window is the
//      ransomware staging pattern and raises severity.
//   The utility list and command fragments follow the T1490 technique page (ATT&CK v19.2), which lists
//   vssadmin, wmic, wbadmin, bcdedit, REAgentC, and diskshadow. diskshadow is the one exception: the
//   query does not classify it (see Known false positives).
//
// Known false positives
//   - Backup and imaging products that manage shadow copies on servers (add their initiating process
//     to ApprovedInitiators after confirming the binary path and signer).
//   - Administrators resizing shadow storage on file servers (RES-FILES) during change windows.
//   - Kiosk or reimaging workflows that set bcdedit recovery options on build hosts.
//   - WinRE maintenance: Microsoft's KB5028997 procedure for resizing the recovery partition runs
//     reagentc /disable and then reagentc /enable. A disable followed by an enable on the same device
//     under a change record is that procedure.
//   - diskshadow in script mode hides its commands from the command line; the query cannot classify it
//     and does not alert on it. Review diskshadow.exe executions separately on servers.
//
// Tuning knobs (let statements below)
//   LookbackWindow       hunting window. Remove the Timestamp filter when saving as a custom detection:
//                        Microsoft documents that the service applies the rule lookback itself.
//   ApprovedInitiators   initiating process names for sanctioned backup or imaging tools.
//   ExcludedDevices      build or imaging hosts where recovery changes are expected.
//
// Response notes (clinical safety gate, ADR-008 and 04-segmentation.md section 3)
//   - Non-medical workstations: device isolation through PEP-HOST is allowed as an automated action.
//     Add the clinical notification step when the device is a shared clinical workstation (Z-CLIN-USER).
//   - Servers in Z-CLIN-APP (for example the RES-EHR application tier): isolation takes a clinical system
//     offline, so the incident commander and the clinical lead decide, and EHR downtime procedures start.
//   - Check SVC-BACKUP immediately: if backups are reachable from the affected host's credentials, treat
//     recovery as at risk and escalate per the identity compromise playbook (Branch D step 5). A copy
//     that production credentials can reach also fails ADR-009 decision 2 (isolation from Tier 0
//     administrative reach), so record it as a finding in its own right, even after containment.
//
// Validation notes
//   Not run against any tenant. To validate, execute recovery-inhibition behaviors on an isolated lab
//   Windows VM that has a restorable snapshot, never on a clinical or production system. Expected
//   telemetry: DeviceProcessEvents rows for vssadmin.exe, wbadmin.exe, bcdedit.exe, or reagentc.exe with
//   the matching command lines, and one output row for the device. If the lab uses PowerShell 7, record
//   the ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName value it shows for pwsh.exe. Public library: Atomic Red Team
//   tests mapped to T1490 (operator selects and runs them; purple-team-plan.md exercise PX-01).
//
// References
//   https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1490/
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-xdr/advanced-hunting-deviceprocessevents-table
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-xdr/custom-detection-rules
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-endpoint/minimum-requirements
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/manufacture/desktop/reagentc-command-line-options
//   https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/topic/kb5028997-instructions-to-manually-resize-your-partition-to-install-the-winre-update-400faa27-9343-461c-ada9-24c8229763bf
//   https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell/issues/14614
//   https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/master/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_reagentc_disable_windows_recovery_environment.yml
//
let LookbackWindow = 1h;
// Sanctioned backup or imaging tools that legitimately manage shadow copies (example placeholder value)
let ApprovedInitiators = dynamic(["example-backup-agent.exe"]);
// Build or imaging hosts where recovery configuration changes are expected (empty by default)
let ExcludedDevices = dynamic([]);
DeviceProcessEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(LookbackWindow)
| where FileName in~ ("vssadmin.exe", "wmic.exe", "wbadmin.exe", "bcdedit.exe", "reagentc.exe", "powershell.exe", "pwsh.exe")
    or ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName in~ ("vssadmin.exe", "wmic.exe", "wbadmin.exe", "bcdedit.exe", "reagentc.exe", "powershell.exe", "pwsh.exe", "pwsh.dll")
| where DeviceName !in~ (ExcludedDevices)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName !in~ (ApprovedInitiators)
// Normalize: prefer the original file name so a renamed utility is still classified; strip quotes.
// For pwsh.exe the original file name is pwsh.dll (PowerShell issue #14614), so the PowerShell branch accepts both.
| extend Image = tolower(iff(isnotempty(ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName), ProcessVersionInfoOriginalFileName, FileName))
| extend Cmd = tolower(replace_string(ProcessCommandLine, "\"", ""))
| extend Behavior = case(
    Image == "vssadmin.exe" and Cmd has "delete" and Cmd has "shadows", "Shadow copy deletion (vssadmin)",
    Image == "vssadmin.exe" and Cmd has "resize" and Cmd has "shadowstorage", "Shadow storage resize (vssadmin)",
    Image == "wmic.exe" and Cmd has "shadowcopy" and Cmd has "delete", "Shadow copy deletion (wmic)",
    Image == "wbadmin.exe" and Cmd has "delete" and (Cmd has "catalog" or Cmd has "systemstatebackup" or Cmd has "backup"), "Backup catalog or backup deletion (wbadmin)",
    Image == "bcdedit.exe" and Cmd has "recoveryenabled" and Cmd has "no", "Automatic recovery disabled (bcdedit)",
    Image == "bcdedit.exe" and Cmd has "bootstatuspolicy" and Cmd has "ignoreallfailures", "Boot failure handling suppressed (bcdedit)",
    Image == "reagentc.exe" and Cmd has "disable", "Windows Recovery Environment disabled (reagentc)",
    Image in ("powershell.exe", "pwsh.exe", "pwsh.dll") and Cmd has "win32_shadowcopy" and (Cmd has "delete" or Cmd contains "remove-ciminstance" or Cmd contains "remove-wmiobject"), "Shadow copy deletion (PowerShell WMI or CIM)",
    "")
| where isnotempty(Behavior)
// One row per device, keeping the latest event's Timestamp, ReportId, and DeviceId for custom detection mapping
| summarize arg_max(Timestamp, ReportId, AccountDomain, AccountName, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, ProcessCommandLine),
    FirstSeen = min(Timestamp),
    DistinctBehaviors = dcount(Behavior),
    Behaviors = make_set(Behavior, 10),
    EventCount = count()
    by DeviceId, DeviceName
| extend SuggestedSeverity = iff(DistinctBehaviors >= 2, "High: multiple recovery-inhibition behaviors on one device", "Medium: single behavior, check change records before closing")
| project Timestamp, ReportId, DeviceId, DeviceName, SuggestedSeverity, Behaviors, DistinctBehaviors, EventCount, FirstSeen,
    AccountDomain, AccountName, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, ProcessCommandLine
